## 3. Computation of ejecta properties from BHs and NSNS mergers

During the final phase of a NSNS merger, tidal forces lead to a partial disruption of the stars, producing an outflow of neutron-rich material. When the crusts of the two NS impact each other, compression, shock heating and potentially neutrino ablation cause an additional outflow (Hotokezaka et al., 2013; Bauswein et al., 2013; Radice et al., 2016; Dietrich et al., 2017; Beloborodov et al., 2018). The released NS material can be divided into two components: the dynamical ejecta, gravitationally unbound, that leave the merger region, and a bound component, which forms an accretion disc around the merger remnant. On longer timescales, other outflows originate from the disc: faster ejecta produced by magnetic pressure and neutrino-matter interaction during the initial neutrino-cooling-dominated accretion phase (we call these "wind ejecta"), and slower but more massive ejecta produced by viscous processes in the disc, especially during the advection-dominated phase (Dessart et al., 2009; Metzger and Fernandez, 2014; Perego et al., 2014; Just et al., 2015; Siegel and Metzger, 2017 - we call these "secular ejecta"). Substantial differences in the ejecta properties arise depending on the post-merger scenario (see i.e. Kawaguchi et al., 2019).

In order to calculate dynamical ejecta and disc mass from a NSNS merger we adopt the fitting formulae reported in Radice et al. (2018), which are calibrated on a large suite of high-resolution GRHD simulations7. Both quantities depend on the NS masses and tidal deformabilities. We also adopt their formula for the dynamical ejecta mass-weighted average asymptotic velocity v_{\\rm dyn}.

Footnote 7: We note that Kiuchi et al. (2019) showed that the predictions from these formulae might underestimate the produced disc mass in binaries with large mass ratios. However they consider the case with M_{\\rm NS,1}=1.55\\,{\\rm M_{\\odot}} and M_{\\rm NS,2}=1.2\\,{\\rm M_{\\odot}}, thus low-mass/largely deformable NSs. Instead, as can be seen in Fig. 2, we consider systems with M_{\\rm NS,1}>1.65\\,{\\rm M_{\\odot}} and M_{\\rm NS,2}>1.35\\,{\\rm M_{\\odot}}. Therefore the NSs in our systems are less deformable and we expect that the underestimation reported in Kiuchi et al. (2019) is less significant.

The NS tidal disruption can occur also in BHNS mergers. If the NS is disrupted outside the innermost stable circular orbit, then the released material remains outside the BH in the form of a crescent (e.g. Kawaguchi et al., 2016), otherwise the NS plunges directly onto the BH. We adopt the fitting formula from Foucart et al. (2018) to calculate the total mass remaining outside the BH, M_{\\rm out}. This quantity depends on the BH mass and spin, and on the NS mass, tidal deformability and baryonic mass M_{\\rm NS}^{\\rm h}. We adopt the formulae in Kawaguchi et al. (2016) to calculate the dynamical ejecta mass and average velocity v_{\\rm dyn} in this case. M_{\\rm dyn} depends on the BH mass and spin, the NS mass, baryonic mass and compactness C_{\\rm NS}, and on the angle \\iota_{\\rm tilt} between the binary total angular momentum and the BH spin. We assume \\iota_{\\rm tilt}=0. v_{\\rm dyn} depends only on the mass ratio q=M_{\\rm BH}/M_{\\rm NS}. We proceed as in Barbieri et al. (2019) to calculate C_{\\rm NS} from \\Lambda_{\\rm NS} and M_{\\rm NS}^{\\rm h} from M_{\\rm NS} and C_{\\rm NS}. We then calculate the disc mass as M_{\\rm disc}=\\max[M_{\\rm out}-M_{\\rm dyn},0]. As in Barbieri et al. (2019) we assume that M_{\\rm dyn} cannot exceed 30%M_{\\rm out}, considering recent BHNS simulations presented in Foucart et al. (2019).

In what follows, we conservatively assume the BH to be non-spinning (\\chi_{\\rm BH}=0), corresponding to the worst condition for ejecta production8.

Footnote 8: among the co-rotating configurations. Indeed the counter-rotating cases (\\chi_{\\rm BH}<0) are the worst conditions in absolute, more often leading to a direct plunge. However counter-rotating configurations are not expected for field binaries but for the dynamically formed ones, that represent a negligible contribution to the merger rate (Ye et al., 2019).

